WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:03.183
Not every threat comes from a tool.

2
00:00:03.183 --> 00:00:08.910
People call, walk in, email, text, chat, or tell us during a video call.

3
00:00:08.910 --> 00:00:17.023
In this demonstration I receive three reports from a school, record each one the way our SOP requires,

4
00:00:17.023 --> 00:00:19.410
and decide what to do with it.

5
00:00:19.410 --> 00:00:21.159
One of them is a trap.

6
00:00:22.259 --> 00:00:26.230
First, which channel did each report come from?

7
00:00:26.230 --> 00:00:31.636
grep with dash E finds the report headers and the CHANNEL lines.

8
00:00:31.636 --> 00:00:35.100
A phone call, an SMS, and a chat message.

9
00:00:36.199 --> 00:00:40.203
Report DR-1, a phone call from the school library.

10
00:00:40.203 --> 00:00:45.008
Grep dash A seven shows the header and the next seven lines.

11
00:00:45.008 --> 00:00:54.216
The caller's Word files now end in dot crypt nine, and a file called HOW TO GET FILES BACK appeared on the desktop.

12
00:00:54.216 --> 00:01:01.263
Files renamed with one new extension, plus a ransom note: those are red flags R1 and R2.

13
00:01:01.263 --> 00:01:02.143
Ransomware.

14
00:01:03.243 --> 00:01:06.552
For a ransomware red flag the SOP is clear.

15
00:01:06.552 --> 00:01:13.938
I ask the caller to unplug the network cable or turn off the Wi-Fi, and not to shut the PC down,

16
00:01:13.938 --> 00:01:17.093
because evidence in memory would be lost.

17
00:01:17.093 --> 00:01:20.017
Never pay, never contact the attacker.

18
00:01:20.017 --> 00:01:24.171
Then I phone the Shift Lead right away, and record it.

19
00:01:25.271 --> 00:01:27.016
Report DR-2 is an SMS.

20
00:01:27.016 --> 00:01:37.007
Someone who says she is Ma'am Tess from the Registrar asks me to reset her password and text her the new password and the OTP.

21
00:01:38.107 --> 00:01:40.646
Rule B3 of the SOP answers this.

22
00:01:40.646 --> 00:01:44.693
Never ask for or give a password or a one-time PIN.

23
00:01:44.693 --> 00:01:50.088
A request like this is itself a possible social engineering attempt.

24
00:01:50.088 --> 00:01:51.596
So I do not comply.

25
00:01:51.596 --> 00:01:59.371
I call back on the number in the school directory, not the number that texted me, and I report it.

26
00:02:00.471 --> 00:02:02.353
Report DR-3, a chat.

27
00:02:02.353 --> 00:02:06.209
The projector in Room 204 has no display.

28
00:02:06.209 --> 00:02:10.912
That is a hardware problem, not a security matter.

29
00:02:10.912 --> 00:02:16.743
Rule B4: record it and redirect the person to the IT Helpdesk.

30
00:02:17.843 --> 00:02:20.276
Here are my three intake records.

31
00:02:20.276 --> 00:02:27.058
For every report I wrote the channel, the reporter and a callback number from the directory,

32
00:02:27.058 --> 00:02:31.628
the affected device, the type, and the next step from the SOP.

33
00:02:31.628 --> 00:02:37.451
I record only the personal data the SOP needs, because of the Data Privacy Act.

34
00:02:38.551 --> 00:02:39.430
Your turn.

35
00:02:39.430 --> 00:02:44.173
In the CTM Virtual Lab, choose Day 5 and Activity 5.2.

36
00:02:44.173 --> 00:02:50.498
There are six reports, one for each channel in the Competency Standards.

37
00:02:50.498 --> 00:02:55.681
Record each one, classify it, and choose the SOP next step.

38
00:02:55.681 --> 00:02:59.458
Two of them match alerts from Activity 5.1.

39
00:02:59.458 --> 00:03:01.039
Can you find them?

